L000256	\section{Methods and samples}
L000257	
L000258	\subsection{Sample growth and fabrication}
L000259	
L000260	The devices, including the simple disk, Corbino disk, and Hall bar structures, were fabricated from the same wafer of a 6-QL chromium-doped $\text{(Bi,Sb)}_2\text{Te}_3$ film grown by molecular beam epitaxy (MBE) on a GaAs (111)B substrate. First, the film was patterned by argon ion milling. Ti/Au (3 nm/47 nm) contact electrodes were then deposited by thermal evaporation. Next, an approximately 200 nm AlO$_x$ gate dielectric was deposited at room temperature using oxygen plasma-assisted atomic layer deposition (ALD), followed by the evaporation of the top gate. All patterning steps were performed using Molybdenum (Mo) masks to avoid the use of solvents. The dielectric layer is much thicker than those in conventional samples to ensure insulation, as the sample area is large. The simple disk-shaped sample has a film radius of 1200 µm, and its top gate overlaps the contact region by 40 µm in width to ensure complete coverage. The Corbino disk-shaped sample (only used in Supplemental Material) has inner and outer radii of 350 µm and 1000 µm, and a partially covered, concentric top gate with inner and outer radii of 450 µm and 900 µm.
L000261	
L000262	\subsection{AC magnetic field generation}
L000263	
L000264	The AC magnetic field was generated by a solenoidal coil wound on a 2 mm-thick cylindrical copper framework thermally anchored to the 1 K still plate. The framework is electrically grounded to shield against capacitive coupling between the coil and sample wires. The coil is constructed using 0.438 mm-diameter Cu-clad NbTi superconducting wire and has a height of 50 mm, an inner diameter of 48 mm, and an inductance of 180 mH. Samples are positioned at the coil center, where the AC field is uniform and perpendicular to the sample plane. The coil is driven by the differential sine outputs of an SR865A lock-in amplifier (50 $\Omega$ output impedance per terminal), providing zero-mean excitation voltage to minimize crosstalk.
L000265	
L000266	The dependence of the amplitude and phase of the AC field on the excitation voltage was calibrated in situ at each measurement frequency using a GaAs/AlGaAs Hall sensor placed near the sample. A 0.01 T DC background field was applied to ensure a linear response. We applied a DC current to pass through the sensor and measured the AC Hall voltage using a lock-in amplifier referenced in-phase with the excitation voltage. The AC field was extracted via the sensor's Hall coefficient ($k_\text{Hall}=\Delta\rho_{yx}/\Delta B$), measured by conventional transport measurements. Currents of $\pm 1$ µA were applied for the calibration, and the results were anti-symmetrized to eliminate background crosstalk. Calibration results are given in Sec. \ref{sec:coil_calibration}.
L000267	
L000268	\subsection{Charge signal measurement with the compensation}
L000269	
L000270	For charge-signal measurements, the contact and top gate of the disk-shaped sample were connected through low-resistance coaxial cables to home-built transimpedance current preamplifiers based on ADA4530-1 chips, with a transimpedance of $R_0=10$ M$\Omega$. The contact line was held at ground potential by grounding the offset input of its preamplifier. The top gate preamplifier output was sent simultaneously to a lock-in amplifier for signal readout and to an RC divider network that generated the compensation voltage, which was fed back to the top gate line through the offset input of the top gate preamplifier. The compensation mechanism, which realizes an effective negative capacitance $-C_1$, is analyzed below using the circuit in Fig.~1(d) of the main text.
L000271	
L000272	The transimpedance stage of the top gate preamplifier converts the input current into an output voltage,
L000273	\begin{equation}
L000274	U_\text{out} = U_\text{in} - I_\text{in} R_0.
L000275	\end{equation}
L000276	The RC divider consists of a variable resistor box $R_x$ (0.1 $\Omega$ setting accuracy) in series with a fixed capacitor $C_x=10$ µF. The feedback voltage ($U_\text{feedback}$) is taken from their junction:
L000277	\begin{equation}
L000278	U_\text{feedback} = \frac{U_\text{out}}{1+i\omega C_x R_x}.
L000279	\end{equation}
L000280	Between the RC-divider output and the offset input of the top gate preamplifier, $U_\text{feedback}$ was first processed by an SR560 voltage preamplifier set in high-pass filter mode (0.3 Hz, 6 dB) with a gain of 100, and then reduced by a 1:100 divider, thereby keeping $U_\text{feedback}$ unchanged while suppressing low-frequency noise and avoiding overload of the feedback loop.
L000281	
L000282	Since the operational amplifier enforces $U_\text{in}=U_\text{feedback}$, one obtains
L000283	\begin{equation}
L000284	U_\text{feedback} = -\frac{I_\text{in}}{i\omega C_1}, \qquad C_1 \equiv \frac{C_x R_x}{R_0},
L000285	\end{equation}
L000286	showing that the feedback acts as an effective tunable capacitance $C_1$ set by $R_x$.
L000287	
L000288	With $I_\text{in}=i\omega Q_\text{a}$, the measured output becomes
L000289	\begin{equation}
L000290	U_\text{out} = - I_\text{in}\!\left(R_0 + \frac{1}{i\omega C_1}\right)
L000291	             = -Q_\text{a}\!\left(i\omega R_0 + \frac{1}{C_1}\right),
L000292	\end{equation}
L000293	so that
L000294	\begin{equation}
L000295	Q_\text{a} = -\frac{U_\text{out}}{i\omega R_0 + 1/C_1}.
L000296	\end{equation}
L000297	
L000298	In the measurements, $R_x$ was chosen according to the calibrated capacitances and the target compensation ratio $\alpha$. Grounding the offset input of the top gate preamplifier provided the raw measurement without compensation. The reference phase of all lock-in amplifiers was aligned with the AC coil excitation voltage.
L000299	
L000300	For DC-field-dependent measurements, we used a fixed-point method to maintain a stable low temperature: each field point was held for 600 s for stabilization, followed by 300 s of data acquisition.
L000301	
L000302	
L000303	\section{Quantitative dissipation model for compensated QAH charge accumulation}
L000304	\label{sec:QAH_charge_accumulation}
L000305	
L000306	Throughout the theoretical sections below, all voltages, currents, and charge densities denote complex amplitudes at angular frequency $\omega$. The corresponding physical signal is understood as the real part of the complex amplitude multiplied by $e^{i\omega t}$, consistent with the convention used in the main text.
L000307	
L000308	We begin from the generalized in-plane transport equation,
L000309	\begin{equation}
L000310	\begin{pmatrix}
L000311	J_x \\
L000312	J_y
L000313	\end{pmatrix}
L000314	=
L000315	\begin{pmatrix}
L000316	\sigma_{xx} & \sigma_{xy} \\
L000317	-\sigma_{xy} & \sigma_{xx}
L000318	\end{pmatrix}
L000319	\left[
L000320	\begin{pmatrix}
L000321	E_x \\
L000322	E_y
L000323	\end{pmatrix}
L000324	+
L000325	\frac{1}{e}
L000326	\begin{pmatrix}
L000327	\partial_x \mu \\
L000328	\partial_y \mu
L000329	\end{pmatrix}
L000330	\right],
L000331	\label{eq:SM_QAH_sigma_tensor}
L000332	\end{equation}
L000333	where $\sigma_{xx}$ and $\sigma_{xy}$ are the longitudinal and Hall conductances of the QAH sample, $\mu$ is the chemical potential, $e$ is the elementary charge, and $E$ is the electric field. A time-dependent perpendicular magnetic field $\mathbf B=B_z\hat{\mathbf z}$ generates an in-plane electric field through Faraday's law,
L000334	\begin{equation}
L000335	-\frac{\partial \mathbf B}{\partial t}=\nabla\times\mathbf E
L000336	\quad\Rightarrow\quad
L000337	\frac{\partial B_z}{\partial t}
L000338	=
L000339	\frac{\partial E_x}{\partial y}-\frac{\partial E_y}{\partial x}.
L000340	\label{eq:SM_QAH_faraday}
L000341	\end{equation}
L000342	The induced charge accumulation density $\eta(\mathbf r)$ obeys the continuity equation,
L000343	\begin{equation}
L000344	\frac{\partial \eta}{\partial t}
L000345	=
L000346	-\nabla\cdot\mathbf J.
L000347	\label{eq:SM_QAH_continuity}
L000348	\end{equation}
L000349	Combining Eqs. \eqref{eq:SM_QAH_sigma_tensor}--\eqref{eq:SM_QAH_continuity}, we obtain
L000350	\begin{equation}
L000351	\frac{\partial \eta}{\partial t}
L000352	=
L000353	\sigma_{xy}\frac{\partial B_z}{\partial t}
L000354	-\sigma_{xx}\nabla\cdot\mathbf E -
L000355	\frac{\sigma_{xx}}{e}\nabla^2\mu
L000356	=
L000357	\sigma_{xy}\frac{\partial B_z}{\partial t}
L000358	+\sigma_{xx}\nabla^2 U -
L000359	\frac{\sigma_{xx}}{e}\nabla^2\mu,
L000360	\label{eq:SM_QAH_eta_general}
L000361	\end{equation}
L000362	where $U(\mathbf{r})$ is the electric potential (voltage) in the sample.
L000363	Here we assume spatially uniform $\sigma_{xx}$ and $\sigma_{xy}$, so that the transverse component of the chemical-potential-gradient current is divergence-free.
L000364	
L000365	Note that the chemical potential $\mu$ is related to the charge density $\eta$ through the Fermi level variation or quantum capacitance (unit area),
L000366	\begin{equation}
L000367	    C_\text{q(unit)} = e^2 \frac{\partial n}{\partial \mu} = -e \frac{\partial \eta}{\partial \mu}.
L000368	\end{equation}
L000369	Therefore Eq.~\eqref{eq:SM_QAH_eta_general} can be written as
L000370	\begin{equation}
L000371	\frac{\partial \eta}{\partial t}
L000372	=
L000373	\sigma_{xy}\frac{\partial B_z}{\partial t}
L000374	+\sigma_{xx}\nabla^2 U + \sigma_{xx}
L000375	\frac{1}{C_\text{q(unit)}}\nabla^2\eta.
L000376	\label{eq:SM_QAH_eta_general2}
L000377	\end{equation}
L000378	
L000379	We now consider the simple disk-shaped sample, for which the top gate fully covers the sample area $A_\text{S}$. Let the top-gate dielectric thickness and dielectric constant be $d_\text{TG}$ and $\varepsilon_\text{TG}$, respectively. The local electrostatic relation between the sample potential $U(\mathbf r)$ and the top-gate potential $U_\text{TG}$ is
L000380	\begin{equation}
L000381	U-U_\text{TG}=\frac{d_\text{TG}}{\varepsilon_\text{TG}}\eta
L000382	=\frac{A_\text{S}}{C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}}\eta,
L000383	\label{eq:SM_QAH_electrostatic}
L000384	\end{equation}
L000385	where
L000386	\[
L000387	C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}=\frac{\varepsilon_\text{TG}A_\text{S}}{d_\text{TG}}.
L000388	\]
L000389	
L000390	\subsection{Uncompensated case}
L000391	
L000392	In the uncompensated case, $U_\text{TG}=0$, so
L000393	\[
L000394	U=\frac{A_\text{S}}{C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}}\eta.
L000395	\]
L000396	For a harmonic magnetic drive $B_z=B_\text{AC}e^{i\omega t}$, Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_QAH_eta_general2} becomes
L000397	\begin{equation}
L000398	\left(1+\frac{1}{k^2}\nabla^2\right)\eta=\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC},
L000399	\qquad
L000400	k^2=\frac{\omega (1/C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}+1/C_\text{q})^{-1}}{i\sigma_{xx}A_\text{S}} = \frac{\omega C_\text{TG}}{i\sigma_{xx}A_\text{S}},
L000401	\label{eq:SM_QAH_pde}
L000402	\end{equation}
L000403	where $C_\text{q} = C_\text{q(unit)} A_\text{S}$ is the total quantum capacitance of the sample. We use the total gate capacitance $C_\text{TG}$ to combine the effects of quantum capacitance and geometric capacitance,
L000404	\begin{equation}
L000405	    C_\text{TG} = (\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{q}})^{-1}.
L000406	    \label{eq:SM_total_capacitance}
L000407	\end{equation}
L000408	
L000409	For a simple disk-shaped sample of radius $r_0$, the finite radial solution is
L000410	\[
L000411	\eta(r)=\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC}+a\,J_0(kr),
L000412	\]
L000413	where $J_0$ is the zeroth-order Bessel function of the first kind. The boundary condition for the sample is the fixed electrochemical potential,
L000414	\begin{equation}
L000415	    \mu^{*}(r_0) = \mu(r_0) - e U(r_0) = \text{Const.}\,,
L000416	\end{equation}
L000417	which can be transformed as
L000418	\begin{equation}
L000419	    \Delta\mu^{*}(r_0) = -e \left[\frac{\eta(r_0)}{C_\text{q(unit)}} +U(r_0)\right] = 0.
L000420	    \label{eq:SM_boundary_condition_direct}
L000421	\end{equation}
L000422	The voltage of the gate ($U_\text{TG}$) determines $\eta(r_0)$ via Eqs. \eqref{eq:SM_boundary_condition_direct} and \eqref{eq:SM_QAH_electrostatic}:
L000423	\begin{equation}
L000424	    \eta(r_0) = - U_\text{TG} \frac{(1/{C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}}+1/{C_\text{q}})^{-1}}{A_\text{S}}
L000425	    = - U_\text{TG} \frac{C_\text{TG}}{A_\text{S}}.
L000426	    \label{eq:SM_boundary_condition}
L000427	\end{equation}
L000428	Since the top gate is grounded, Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_boundary_condition} gives $\eta(r_0)=0$, and hence
L000429	\begin{equation}
L000430	\eta(r)=\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC}\left[1-\frac{J_0(kr)}{J_0(kr_0)}\right].
L000431	\label{eq:SM_QAH_eta_raw}
L000432	\end{equation}
L000433	
L000434	The total accumulated charge is
L000435	\begin{equation}
L000436	Q_\text{a}^{\rm raw}
L000437	=
L000438	2\pi\int_0^{r_0}r\,\eta(r)\,dr
L000439	=
L000440	Q_\text{a}^{(0)}(1-\gamma),
L000441	\label{eq:SM_QAH_Qraw}
L000442	\end{equation}
L000443	where
L000444	\begin{equation}
L000445	Q_\text{a}^{(0)}=A_\text{S}\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC}
L000446	\label{eq:SM_QAH_Q0}
L000447	\end{equation}
L000448	is the intrinsic QAH charge, and
L000449	\begin{equation}
L000450	\gamma=\frac{2J_1(kr_0)}{kr_0J_0(kr_0)}
L000451	\label{eq:SM_QAH_gamma}
L000452	\end{equation}
L000453	is the complex attenuation factor, where $J_1$ is the first-order Bessel function of the first kind. Thus $\gamma$ is determined by $\omega$, $\sigma_{xx}$, $C_\text{TG}$, and $A_\text{S}$ (or $r_0$).
L000454	
L000455	\subsection{Compensated case}
L000456	
L000457	We now include the active capacitive compensation. As described in the main text, the compensation circuit is equivalent to a feedback voltage
L000458	\begin{equation}
L000459	U_\text{TG}=-\frac{Q_\text{a}}{C_1},
L000460	\qquad
L000461	\alpha\equiv \frac{C_\text{TG}}{C_1},
L000462	\label{eq:SM_QAH_feedback}
L000463	\end{equation}
L000464	where $C_1$ is the feedback capacitance and $\alpha$ is the compensation ratio.
L000465	
L000466	The differential equation remains Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_QAH_pde}; only the boundary condition is modified. Using Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_boundary_condition}, the feedback $U_\text{TG}$ gives
L000467	\begin{equation}
L000468	\eta(r_0)=\frac{C_\text{TG}}{A_\text{S}C_1}Q_\text{a}
L000469	=\frac{\alpha}{A_\text{S}}Q_\text{a}.
L000470	\label{eq:SM_QAH_boundary_comp}
L000471	\end{equation}
L000472	Integrating the corresponding solution over the sample area yields
L000473	\begin{equation}
L000474	Q_\text{a}
L000475	=
L000476	Q_\text{a}^{(0)}+\left(\alpha Q_\text{a}-Q_\text{a}^{(0)}\right)\gamma,
L000477	\label{eq:SM_QAH_selfconsistent}
L000478	\end{equation}
L000479	from which
L000480	\begin{equation}
L000481	Q_\text{a}^{\rm comp}
L000482	=
L000483	\frac{1-\gamma}{1-\alpha\gamma}\,
L000484	Q_\text{a}^{(0)}.
L000485	\label{eq:SM_QAH_comp_final}
L000486	\end{equation}
L000487	Equivalently, the measured average charge density is
L000488	\begin{equation}
L000489	\eta_\text{a}^{\rm comp}
L000490	=
L000491	\frac{Q_\text{a}^{\rm comp}}{A_\text{S}}
L000492	=
L000493	\frac{1-\gamma}{1-\alpha\gamma}\,
L000494	\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC}.
L000495	\label{eq:SM_QAH_eta_comp_final}
L000496	\end{equation}
L000497	This result is the quantitative dissipation model used in the main text [Fig.~2]. In the uncompensated limit $\alpha=0$, it reduces to Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_QAH_Qraw}; in the full-compensation limit $\alpha=1$, one has
L000498	\[
L000499	Q_\text{a}^{\rm comp}=Q_\text{a}^{(0)},
L000500	\qquad
L000501	\eta_\text{a}^{\rm comp}=\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC}.
L000502	\]
L000503	
L000504	The corresponding electrochemical driving potential ($V^{*} = \Delta \mu^{*}/(-e)$) is
L000505	\begin{equation}
L000506	V^{*}(r)
L000507	=
L000508	\frac{Q_\text{a}^{(0)}}{C_\text{TG}}
L000509	\frac{1-\alpha}{1-\alpha\gamma}
L000510	\left[
L000511	1-\frac{J_0(kr)}{J_0(kr_0)}
L000512	\right].
L000513	\label{eq:SM_QAH_Ur}
L000514	\end{equation}
L000515	At $\alpha=1$, $V^{*}(r)=0$ everywhere, i.e. $\mu^{*}$ is spatially uniform, with no charge diffusion current.
L000516	
L000517	\subsection{Generalization to arbitrary sample shape}
L000518	
L000519	The result is not restricted to the disk geometry. For an arbitrary sample shape with uniformly gated area $A_\text{S}$ and grounded sample boundary, the same differential equation,
L000520	\[
L000521	\left(1+\frac{1}{k^2}\nabla^2\right)\eta=\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC},
L000522	\]
L000523	still applies. Writing
L000524	\[
L000525	\eta(\mathbf r)=\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC}+\xi(\mathbf r),
L000526	\]
L000527	one obtains
L000528	\[
L000529	\left(1+\frac{1}{k^2}\nabla^2\right)\xi=0.
L000530	\]
L000531	Since the sample edge is grounded, the boundary value of $\eta$ is still consistently $\alpha Q_\text{a}/A_\text{S}$ as in Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_QAH_boundary_comp}. By linearity, the solution can therefore be written as
L000532	\[
L000533	\xi(\mathbf r)=\left(\frac{\alpha Q_\text{a}}{A_\text{S}}-\sigma_{xy}B_\text{AC}\right)h(\mathbf r),
L000534	\]
L000535	where $h(\mathbf r)$ is the normalized solution satisfying $h|_{\partial\Omega}=1$. Defining
L000536	\begin{equation}
L000537	\gamma=\frac{1}{A_\text{S}}\int_{A_\text{S}} h(\mathbf r)\,dS,
L000538	\label{eq:SM_QAH_gamma_general}
L000539	\end{equation}
L000540	one again arrives at Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_QAH_selfconsistent}, and hence
L000541	\begin{equation}
L000542	Q_\text{a}^{\rm comp}
L000543	=
L000544	\frac{1-\gamma}{1-\alpha\gamma}\,
L000545	Q_\text{a}^{(0)}.
L000546	\label{eq:SM_QAH_general_final}
L000547	\end{equation}
L000548	Therefore, the dependence on $\alpha$ is universal, while the sample geometry enters only through the complex attenuation factor $\gamma$.
L000549	
L000550	\section{Impedance between the top gate and contact in the simple disk-shaped sample}
L000551	
L000552	We now consider the impedance between the top gate and the grounded sample contact, which is used in Sec. \ref{sec:sigma_xx_calibration} for $\sigma_{xx}$ calibration. This problem is governed by the same dissipative electrodynamics as in Sec. \ref{sec:QAH_charge_accumulation}, with the only difference that $B_\text{AC}=0$. The local electrostatic relation remains
L000553	\begin{equation}
L000554	U-U_t=\frac{d_\text{TG}}{\varepsilon_\text{TG}}\eta
L000555	=\frac{A_\text{S}}{C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}}\eta,
L000556	\label{eq:SM_imp_electrostatic}
L000557	\end{equation}
L000558	where $U_t$ is the voltage applied to the top gate ($U_\text{TG} = U_t$) and $\eta(\mathbf r)$ is the induced charge density on the sample. The governing equation is directly obtained from Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_QAH_pde}:
L000559	\begin{equation}
L000560	\nabla^2\eta + k^2\eta=0,
L000561	\qquad
L000562	k^2=\frac{\omega C_\text{TG}}{i\sigma_{xx}A_\text{S}}.
L000563	\label{eq:SM_imp_pde}
L000564	\end{equation}
L000565	
L000566	For a circular sample of radius $r_0$, the finite radial solution is
L000567	\[
L000568	\eta(r)=a\,J_0(kr),
L000569	\]
L000570	The boundary condition is the same as Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_boundary_condition},
L000571	\[
L000572	-U_t=\frac{A_\text{S}}{C_\text{TG}}\eta(r_0).
L000573	\]
L000574	Therefore,
L000575	\begin{equation}
L000576	\eta(r)=-\frac{C_\text{TG}}{A_\text{S}}U_t\,
L000577	\frac{J_0(kr)}{J_0(kr_0)}.
L000578	\label{eq:SM_imp_eta}
L000579	\end{equation}
L000580	
L000581	Integrating over the sample area, the total charge on the sample is
L000582	\begin{equation}
L000583	Q_\text{s}
L000584	=
L000585	- C_\text{TG}U_t\,
L000586	\frac{2J_1(kr_0)}{kr_0J_0(kr_0)},
L000587	\label{eq:SM_imp_Qs}
L000588	\end{equation}
L000589	and the top-gate charge is
L000590	\begin{equation}
L000591	Q_t=-Q_\text{s}.
L000592	\label{eq:SM_imp_Qt}
L000593	\end{equation}
L000594	The measured impedance is then
L000595	\begin{equation}
L000596	Z_t(\omega)= \frac{U_t}{i\omega Q_t}
L000597	=
L000598	\frac{1}{i\omega C_\text{TG}}
L000599	\frac{kr_0J_0(kr_0)}{2J_1(kr_0)}.
L000600	\label{eq:SM_imp_Z}
L000601	\end{equation}
L000602	
L000603	The parasitic capacitance between the overlapped top gate and contact, $C_\text{p1}$, contributes an additional parallel branch. The overall measured impedance is therefore
L000604	\begin{equation}
L000605	Z_\text{TC}
L000606	=
L000607	\frac{1}{i\omega}
L000608	\left[
L000609	C_\text{p1}+C_\text{TG}\frac{2J_1(kr_0)}{kr_0J_0(kr_0)}
L000610	\right]^{-1}.
L000611	\label{eq:overall_impedance}
L000612	\end{equation}
L000613	For fixed sample geometry, $Z_\text{TC}$ is determined by $\omega$, $\sigma_{xx}$, $C_\text{TG}$, and $C_\text{p1}$. Equation \eqref{eq:overall_impedance} is used in Sec. \ref{sec:sigma_xx_calibration} to calibrate $\sigma_{xx}$.
L000614	
L000615	\section{Equivalent-circuit derivation for the dual-gate TME setup}
L000616	
L000617	We next derive the geometric attenuation factor $\gamma_\text{geo}$ for the dual-gate TME polarization measurement. This is the main attenuation factor of the TME signal and is directly controlled by the compensation method.
L000618	
L000619	The effective circuit of dual-gate measurement is shown in Fig.~4(b) of the main text, which contains the top-gate geometric capacitance $C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}$, the bottom-gate geometric capacitance $C_\text{BG}^\text{(geo)}$, and the sample geometric capacitance $C_\text{S}$ between the top and bottom surfaces. Since $\gamma_\text{geo}$ characterizes how efficiently the TME polarization charge induces an image charge on the gate electrode through the gate dielectric, it is a purely electrostatic capacitive effect and is therefore independent of the quantum capacitance of the sample. However, to avoid introducing excessive definitions, we use $C_\text{TG}$ and $C_\text{BG}$ in Fig.~4(b) to denote $C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}$ and $C_\text{BG}^\text{(geo)}$, respectively. To maintain consistency with the analysis in Fig.~4(b), we continue to use $C_\text{TG}$ and $C_\text{BG}$ in this section to refer to geometric capacitances. In other sections of the Supplemental Material, however, $C_\text{TG}$ and $C_\text{BG}$ are defined including the quantum capacitance contribution (Eq.~\eqref{eq:SM_total_capacitance}). Moreover, based on previous studies \cite{inhofer2017, inhofer2018, dartiailh2020}, the quantum capacitance of topological-insulator systems is typically on the order of 10 mF/m$^2$, which is much larger than the typical geometric capacitance per unit area in our experiment, approximately 0.3 mF/m$^2$. This large difference indicates that, even when the quantum capacitance is included, $C_\text{TG}$ and $C_\text{BG}$ remain approximately equal to $C_\text{TG}^\text{(geo)}$ and $C_\text{BG}^\text{(geo)}$, respectively. Therefore, this simplification is well justified.
L000620	
L000621	The intrinsic TME polarization produces opposite surface charges $\pm Q_\text{4D-QHE}$, with
L000622	\[
L000623	Q_\text{4D-QHE}=A_\text{S}B_\text{AC}\frac{e^2}{2h}N_\text{Ch}^{(2)},
L000624	\]
L000625	which are considered source charges in the circuit. Let $q_1$ and $q_2$ denote the charges measured in the top and bottom gate lines, respectively.
L000626	
L000627	For the uncompensated case, $q_1$, $q_2$, and $q_\text{S}$ denote the charges on the lower plates (see Fig.~4(b) of the main text) of $C_\text{TG}$, $C_\text{BG}$, and $C_\text{S}$, respectively. Since the top and bottom current preamplifiers are both effectively grounded, the voltages on the top and bottom gates are zero, so the three capacitors form a closed series loop. Charge conservation at the top and bottom sample surfaces gives
L000628	\[
L000629	Q_\text{4D-QHE} = q_1 - q_\text{S},
L000630	\qquad
L000631	- Q_\text{4D-QHE} = q_\text{S} - q_2.
L000632	\]
L000633	The total voltage drop across the three capacitors must be zero:
L000634	\begin{equation}
L000635	\frac{q_1}{C_\text{TG}}+\frac{q_\text{S}}{C_\text{S}}+\frac{q_2}{C_\text{BG}}=0.
L000636	\label{eq:SM_TME_voltage_loop}
L000637	\end{equation}
L000638	Solving these equations:
L000639	\begin{equation}
L000640	q_1=q_2=\frac{C_\text{total}}{C_\text{S}}\,Q_\text{4D-QHE} \equiv  \gamma_\text{geo}Q_\text{4D-QHE},
L000641	\qquad
L000642	C_\text{total}
L000643	\equiv
L000644	\left(
L000645	\frac{1}{C_\text{S}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{BG}}
L000646	\right)^{-1}.
L000647	\label{eq:SM_TME_uncomp}
L000648	\end{equation}
L000649	The corresponding geometric attenuation factor is
L000650	\begin{equation}
L000651	\gamma_\text{geo}=\frac{C_\text{total}}{C_\text{S}}.
L000652	\label{eq:SM_TME_gamma_uncomp}
L000653	\end{equation}
L000654	
L000655	When the compensation is applied, the top and bottom gate capacitances are replaced by the effective capacitances
L000656	\begin{equation}
L000657	C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}=\left(\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}}-\frac{1}{C_1}\right)^{-1},
L000658	\qquad
L000659	C_\text{BG}^{\rm eff}=\left(\frac{1}{C_\text{BG}}-\frac{1}{C_2}\right)^{-1}.
L000660	\label{eq:effective_gate_capacitance}
L000661	\end{equation}
L000662	Therefore, Eq. \eqref{eq:SM_TME_uncomp} remains valid after the substitutions
L000663	\[
L000664	C_\text{TG}\rightarrow C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff},
L000665	\qquad
L000666	C_\text{BG}\rightarrow C_\text{BG}^{\rm eff}.
L000667	\]
L000668	Namely,
L000669	\begin{equation}
L000670	C_\text{total}^{\rm eff}
L000671	=
L000672	\left(
L000673	\frac{1}{C_\text{S}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{BG}^{\rm eff}}
L000674	\right)^{-1},
L000675	\qquad
L000676	\gamma_\text{geo}=\frac{C_\text{total}^{\rm eff}}{C_\text{S}},
L000677	\qquad
L000678	q_1=q_2=\gamma_\text{geo}Q_\text{4D-QHE}.
L000679	\label{eq:SM_TME_gamma_eff}
L000680	\end{equation}
L000681	Thus, the role of the compensation is to increase $C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}$ and $C_\text{BG}^{\rm eff}$, and hence to increase $\gamma_\text{geo}$ toward unity.
L000682	
L000683	To relate this to the notation used in the main text, we define the compensation ratio
L000684	\begin{equation}
L000685	\alpha
L000686	=
L000687	\frac{\frac{1}{C_1}+\frac{1}{C_2}}
L000688	{\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{BG}}}.
L000689	\label{eq:SM_TME_alpha}
L000690	\end{equation}
L000691	Then
L000692	\[
L000693	\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{BG}^{\rm eff}}
L000694	=
L000695	(1-\alpha)\left(\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{BG}}\right),
L000696	\]
L000697	so that
L000698	\begin{equation}
L000699	\gamma_\text{geo}
L000700	=
L000701	\frac{1}{
L000702	1+C_\text{S}(1-\alpha)\left(\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}}+\frac{1}{C_\text{BG}}\right)
L000703	}.
L000704	\label{eq:SM_TME_gamma_alpha}
L000705	\end{equation}
L000706	
L000707	Equations \eqref{eq:SM_TME_uncomp}--\eqref{eq:SM_TME_gamma_alpha} are the key results of the dual-gate TME circuit analysis. They show that $\gamma_\text{geo}$ is the dominant electrostatic attenuation factor of the TME measurement and that it is directly tuned by the compensation through the effective gate capacitances.
L000708	
L000709	\section{Dissipation time scale analysis}
L000710	
L000711	The dissipation time scales can be understood from a simple RC-decay picture. In both the QAH and TME measurements, the relaxation is governed by the longitudinal conduction of the sample, so the effective resistance scales as
L000712	\[
L000713	R\sim \frac{1}{\sigma_{xx}}.
L000714	\]
L000715	
L000716	For the compensated QAH charge accumulation, the sample voltage is approximately
L000717	\[
L000718	U_\text{QAH} \approx Q_\text{a}\left(\frac{1}{C_\text{TG}}-\frac{1}{C_1}\right)=\frac{Q_\text{a}}{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}.
L000719	\]
L000720	The corresponding time scale is
L000721	\begin{equation}
L000722	\tau_\text{QAH}\sim R\,C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}\sim \frac{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}{\sigma_{xx}}.
L000723	\label{eq:SM_tau_QAH}
L000724	\end{equation}
L000725	For symmetric single-gate compensation, $C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}=C_\text{TG}/(1-\alpha)$, so equivalently
L000726	\[
L000727	\tau_\text{QAH}\sim \frac{C_\text{TG}}{\sigma_{xx}(1-\alpha)}.
L000728	\]
L000729	
L000730	For the TME setup, the measurable gate charge is reduced by the geometric factor,
L000731	\[
L000732	q_1=q_2=\gamma_\text{geo}Q_\text{4D-QHE}.
L000733	\]
L000734	The corresponding surface voltage is
L000735	\[
L000736	U_\text{TME}=\frac{q_1}{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}
L000737	=\frac{\gamma_\text{geo}Q_\text{4D-QHE}}{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}.
L000738	\]
L000739	Comparing this with the QAH voltage scale $U_\text{QAH}$ under the same $\sigma_{xx}$,
L000740	\begin{equation}
L000741	\frac{\tau_\text{TME}}{\tau_\text{QAH}} = \frac{1}{\gamma_\text{geo}}.
L000742	\label{eq:SM_tau_ratio}
L000743	\end{equation}
L000744	Therefore,
L000745	\begin{equation}
L000746	\tau_\text{TME}
L000747	\sim
L000748	\frac{\tau_\text{QAH}}{\gamma_\text{geo}}
L000749	=
L000750	\frac{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}{\sigma_{xx}}
L000751	\frac{C_\text{S}}{C_\text{total}^{\rm eff}}.
L000752	\label{eq:SM_tau_TME}
L000753	\end{equation}
L000754	
L000755	In summary,
L000756	\begin{equation}
L000757	\tau_\text{QAH}\sim \frac{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}{\sigma_{xx}},
L000758	\qquad
L000759	\tau_\text{TME}\sim \frac{C_\text{TG}^{\rm eff}}{\sigma_{xx}}\frac{C_\text{S}}{C_\text{total}^{\rm eff}}
L000760	=\frac{\tau_\text{QAH}}{\gamma_\text{geo}}.
L000761	\label{eq:SM_tau_summary}
L000762	\end{equation}
L000763	
